|~ The command line: its topic, `::cli`, and the names it declares
   under the kinds of their subjects, `table` under `::vec` and the names
   of the streams under `::any`, each with the parts of its object; their
   primitives, in `cli/src/io-operands.mjs` and `format-operands.mjs`,
   are plain functions over the served subject and the parts. ~|

:cli
  |~~ The command line of qlang: `@in`, `@out`, `@err` and `@tap` read
      and write the streams of the process, and `table` renders a
      vector of maps as text. A value one query hands another over a
      pipe keeps its kinds as its literal: `print | @out` writes it and
      `@in | parse | open` reads it back.
      A query runs on what stdin holds: JSON read as values, an object
      a map whose keys are keywords, whose answer is written as JSON,
      where a value JSON has no form for is refused; text read
      as a string, whose answer is written as its `print`; and
      `| json` writes JSON text. A query that writes to stdout itself,
      by `@out`, holds the channel, and its answer is left unwritten.
      Its verbs are declared under the kinds of their subjects, a query
      calls them by their names, and the manifest of each kind lists
      them:
      ~(::vec | manifest | has ::vec/table)
      ~(::any | manifest | has ::any/@out)
  ~~|

:cli/AnswerNotJsonError
  |~~ The input was JSON, so the answer is written as JSON, and a value
      under it has no form there: a quote, a tag or a value under a tag;
      a set is written as the array of its elements, and a keyword as
      its name.
      `:path` holds the map keys and the indices to it, `:actualType` its
      kind. The query ends before the answer is data: `* (…)` runs a body
      where `* ~(…)` hands each element the quote, and `payload` a value
      without its tag.
  ~~|

:cli/AnswerNotJsonError/path
  |~~ The path to the value JSON has no form for. ~~|
  ::vec

:any/@in
  |~~ The text stdin holds, read to its end whatever the subject, the
      text the command line also lifts into the subject; a terminal with
      nothing piped answers the empty string. It heads a query that reads
      the text as it came: ::snippet~(@in | lines | count) counts the
      lines piped in.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/@in/text
  |~~ The text stdin held, the empty string when nothing was piped. ~~|
  ::string

:any/@out
  |~~ Writes a line to stdout and answers its subject as it came: the
      subject when it is a string, `"hi" | @out`, or the text a modifier
      computes from it, `42 | @out json`, the steps after still reading
      `42`. A query that writes by `@out` leaves its answer unwritten.
      ::snippet~([1 2] | @out json | count) writes `[1,2]`.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/@out/text
  |~~ The text the call gives, null when it gives none.

      The text `@out` writes is a string; a modifier that answers
      another kind is refused, and `json` or `print` makes the text.
  ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:any/@out/text/NotStringOrNullError
  |~~ The text is a string or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::string ::null)

:any/OutSubjectNotStringError
  |~~ `@out` alone writes its subject, a string, as a line; another
      value is written as the text a modifier computes from it,
      `42 | @out json` or `{:x 1} | @out print`.
  ~~|

:any/@err
  |~~ Writes a line to stderr, the channel of diagnostics, as `@out`
      writes to stdout, and answers its subject; the answer of the query
      is written as ever.
      ::snippet~([1 2 3] | @err "counting" | count) writes `counting` to
      stderr and answers 3.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/@err/text
  |~~ The text the call gives, null when it gives none.

      The text `@err` writes is a string; `json` or `print` makes one
      of any value.
  ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:any/@err/text/NotStringOrNullError
  |~~ The text is a string or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::string ::null)

:any/ErrSubjectNotStringError
  |~~ `@err` alone writes its subject, a string, as a line on stderr;
      another value is written as the text a modifier computes, `@err
      json` or `@err print`.
  ~~|

:any/@tap
  |~~ Writes `[tap <label>]` and the literal of its subject to stderr and
      answers the subject, a look at a value halfway through a query:
      ::snippet~([{:on true} {:on false}] | @tap :before | filter ~(/on) | @tap :after | count)
      shows both vectors and answers 1.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:any/@tap/label
  |~~ The label the call gives.

      The label of `@tap` is a keyword, `@tap :before`, written before
      the value as `[tap before]`.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:any/@tap/label/NotKeywordError
  |~~ The label is a keyword. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::keyword)

:vec/table
  |~~ A vector of maps drawn as a table of fixed-width columns, a view
      for a terminal. Columns are derived from keys in first-occurrence
      order across all rows; missing keys render as empty cells. A
      string cell prints bare, null as an empty cell, and every other
      value as its literal on one line. The empty vector yields the marker
      "(empty)".

      ~([{:name "alice" :age 30} {:name "bob" :age 25}] | table | contains "alice")
      ~([{:at {:file "A.java" :line 12}}] | table | contains "{:file \"A.java\" :line 12}")
      ~([] | table | eq "(empty)")

      `table` draws a vector of maps, a row each; a single map goes in a
      vector first, `[/] | table`.
      ~(
        42 | table
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::vec/table/SubjectNotVecError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:vec/table/drawn
  |~~ The rows drawn as text, a line each. ~~|
  ::string

:vec/TableRowNotMapError
  |~~ Every row of `table` is a map, its keys the columns; `:index`
      names the first that is not.
      ~(
        [42 "hello"] | table
        !| [type /index /actualType]
        |  eq [::vec/TableRowNotMapError 0 ::number]
       )
  ~~|
